\title{Sonic Mathematics: Local Counterpoint Rules and Their Generated Thin Category}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a precise categorical model of two-voice, first-species counterpoint. A dyad is an ordered pair of integer pitches, and its vertical interval is the upper pitch minus the lower pitch. The allowed simple consonances are the absolute semitone distances $0,3,4,7,8,9,12$; the perfect consonances are $0,7,12$. A one-step motion is permitted when both endpoint dyads are consonant, each voice moves by at most two semitones, and similar motion does not connect two perfect consonances. We prove that every consonant dyad admits a stationary permitted motion, but that the permitted-motion relation is not transitive. An explicit chain of parallel minor thirds gives two legal steps whose composite displacement is not stepwise. Therefore raw one-step motions cannot be exactly the morphisms of a thin category or comparisons of a preorder.

We repair this failure by passing to finite-path reachability. The reflexive-transitive closure is a preorder and hence defines a thin category whose morphisms record existence of legal paths. Restricting to seven canonical interval representatives with stationary bass gives exactly fifteen directed one-step motions, including identities. The minor and major thirds are mutually connected, as are the perfect fifth and both sixths, so generated reachability is generally not a partial order until mutual reachability is quotiented. The explicit model has seven objects rather than twelve, demonstrating that a twelve-state conjecture requires additional musical state.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Counterpoint organizes simultaneous melodic lines through local constraints and global goals. In first species, one note is placed against one note, making the relation between adjacent dyads particularly transparent. This invites a graph description: consonant sonorities are vertices and permitted transitions are directed edges. It also invites a categorical description: sonorities are objects, voice leadings are morphisms, and concatenation is composition.

The categorical description is stronger. A directed graph imposes no condition on consecutive edges, whereas a category requires their composite. If morphisms are precisely legal single-step motions, the local relation must be reflexive and transitive in the thin case. Reflexivity is natural: a stationary consonance violates neither step size nor parallel-motion restrictions. Transitivity is not: two small steps may accumulate into a leap too large to count as one step.

We test two claims suggested by a twelve-object thin-category model of first-species counterpoint. First, can immediate legal motions be the comparisons of a preorder? Second, does the selected interval state space contain twelve objects? The answers for the explicit model below are no and seven, respectively. These negative results identify the appropriate positive construction: the finite-path reachability category generated by the local graph.

Our principal conclusions are that stationary motions are permitted; one-step motion is nontransitive; no preorder has exactly those one-step comparisons; finite-path reachability is a preorder and determines a thin category; the standard simple consonances have seven distinct realizations; and the canonical motion table has fifteen directed edges, with nontrivial mutual reachability.

\section{Definitions}
\subsection{Dyads and consonance}
Pitches are integers measured in semitones. A \emph{dyad} is $x=(x_0,x_1)\in\Z^2$, where the coordinates name the lower and upper voices. No inequality between coordinates is required.

\begin{definition}[Vertical interval]
The directed vertical interval of $x$ is
\[
\nu(x)=x_1-x_0.
\]
\end{definition}

Let
\[
C=\{0,3,4,7,8,9,12\},\qquad P=\{0,7,12\}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Consonance and perfection]
An integer interval $k$ is \emph{consonant} when $|k|\in C$, and \emph{perfect} when $|k|\in P$.
\end{definition}

Thus the simple consonances are unison, minor third, major third, perfect fifth, minor sixth, major sixth, and octave. The perfect consonances are unison, fifth, and octave. Diatonic spelling is not represented.

\subsection{Local motion}
\begin{definition}[Stepwise motion]
A motion from $x$ to $y$ is stepwise when
\[
|y_0-x_0|\le2\quad\text{and}\quad |y_1-x_1|\le2.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Similar motion]
The motion is similar when both voices move strictly in the same direction:
\[
(x_0<y_0\text{ and }x_1<y_1)\quad\text{or}\quad
(y_0<x_0\text{ and }y_1<x_1).
\]
A stationary voice therefore makes motion oblique rather than similar.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Permitted one-step motion]
Write $M(x,y)$ when both endpoint intervals are consonant, the motion is stepwise, and it is not similar motion between two perfect consonances. Equivalently,
\begin{align*}
M(x,y)\Longleftrightarrow{}& |\nu(x)|\in C\ \land\ |\nu(y)|\in C\\
&\land\ |y_0-x_0|\le2\ \land\ |y_1-x_1|\le2\\
&\land\neg\bigl(|\nu(x)|\in P\land |\nu(y)|\in P
\land \operatorname{Similar}(x,y)\bigr).
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

This is a transparent test model, not a complete codification of historical practice. It omits duration, diatonic spelling, fixed cantus, register, long-range melody, and boundary formulas.

\subsection{Thin categories}
A category is \emph{thin} if every hom-set has at most one member. A preorder $(X,\preceq)$ defines a thin category: a unique arrow $x\to y$ exists precisely when $x\preceq y$. Reflexivity supplies identities and transitivity supplies composition. Conversely, arrow existence in a thin category is a preorder. A partial order also requires antisymmetry. Every preorder admits a poset quotient by
\[
x\sim y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\preceq y\text{ and }y\preceq x.
\]

\section{The local obstruction}
\begin{theorem}[Stationary-motion theorem]
If $x$ is a consonant dyad, then $M(x,x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both endpoints are the same consonant dyad. Each voice moves by zero, satisfying the stepwise bound. Similar motion requires strict movement in one direction, so stationary motion is not similar. The forbidden conjunction is false, even when $x$ is perfect.
\end{proof}

Thus the local relation has identity candidates. Composition is the obstruction.

\begin{theorem}[Nontransitivity of permitted motion]
There exist dyads $x,y,z$ such that $M(x,y)$ and $M(y,z)$ hold but $M(x,z)$ fails.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set
\[
x=(0,3),\qquad y=(2,5),\qquad z=(4,7).
\]
Every dyad has vertical interval $3$, hence is an imperfect consonance. In each adjacent motion, both voices rise by two semitones. The motions are stepwise. Although they are similar, their intervals are not perfect, so they are permitted. Directly from $x$ to $z$, each voice rises by four semitones, violating the stepwise bound. Thus the first two relations hold and the third fails.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No exact local preorder]
There is no preorder $\preceq$ on all dyads for which $x\preceq y$ if and only if $M(x,y)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The witness above would give $x\preceq y$ and $y\preceq z$. Transitivity would imply $x\preceq z$, hence $M(x,z)$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No exact thin category of one-step motions]
No thin category on the dyads has morphism existence exactly equal to $M$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Morphism existence is transitive under composition, while $M$ is not.
\end{proof}

The failure comes from bounded displacement: two legal displacements bounded by two semitones can sum to four.

\section{The generated counterpoint category}
\begin{definition}[Finite-path reachability]
Write $x\leadstoM y$ when there are $r\ge0$ and dyads
\[
x=x^{(0)},x^{(1)},\ldots,x^{(r)}=y
\]
such that $M(x^{(i)},x^{(i+1)})$ for every $0\le i<r$. The case $r=0$ is allowed.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Reachability preorder]
The relation $\leadstoM$ is reflexive and transitive.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The empty path proves $x\leadstoM x$. Concatenating a path from $x$ to $y$ and a path from $y$ to $z$ gives a path from $x$ to $z$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Generated thin category]
Objects are dyads. A morphism $x\to y$ exists exactly when $x\leadstoM y$, and all paths with fixed endpoints determine the same morphism.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Thinness and path semantics]
Between fixed dyads $x$ and $y$ there is at most one generated morphism, and it exists exactly when a finite permitted path connects $x$ to $y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Existence is by definition. Reflexivity and transitivity provide identities and composition. Declaring one arrow per reachable pair makes each hom-set empty or a singleton.
\end{proof}

Every direct permitted motion is a path of length one. The converse fails: the nontransitivity witness gives a two-step generated arrow without a direct permitted motion. The thin category forgets route identity. If routes matter, the free path category should be used instead, with paths as morphisms and concatenation as composition.

\section{The canonical seven-consonance model}
Let $S$ consist of unison, minor third, major third, perfect fifth, minor sixth, major sixth, and octave. Define the realization $s:S\to\N$ by the corresponding list
\[
0,3,4,7,8,9,12.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Seven-object theorem]
The set $S$ has cardinality seven, and $s$ is injective. In particular, $S$ does not have cardinality twelve.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The seven named constructors are distinct, as are their displayed integer images. Thus $s$ is injective and $|S|=7\ne12$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Consonant realization]
For every $i\in S$, the realization $s(i)$ is consonant.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every displayed value belongs to $C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Canonical dyad and motion]
For $i\in S$, let $d(i)=(0,s(i))$. Canonical motion from $i$ to $j$ is permitted when $M(d(i),d(j))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Canonical adjacency criterion]
For $i,j\in S$, canonical motion from $i$ to $j$ is permitted if and only if
\[
|s(j)-s(i)|\le2.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The bass displacement is zero, while upper displacement is $|s(j)-s(i)|$. Both endpoints are consonant. Because the bass is stationary, motion is not similar; hence the perfect-consonance prohibition cannot apply. Only the displayed upper-voice bound remains.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fifteen-motion theorem]
Exactly fifteen ordered canonical one-step motions are permitted, including identities.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The adjacency criterion gives seven identities. The distinct unordered pairs at distance at most two are
\[
\{3,4\},\quad\{7,8\},\quad\{7,9\},\quad\{8,9\}.
\]
Each contributes two directions. No other distinct pair qualifies, so the total is $7+2\cdot4=15$.
\end{proof}

The complete table is
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\toprule
Source & Permitted targets\\
\midrule
$0$ & $0$\\
$3$ & $3,4$\\
$4$ & $3,4$\\
$7$ & $7,8,9$\\
$8$ & $7,8,9$\\
$9$ & $7,8,9$\\
$12$ & $12$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{theorem}[Reversible thirds]
Canonical motion is permitted from minor third to major third and from major third to minor third.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The semitone realizations $3$ and $4$ differ by one. Apply the canonical adjacency criterion in both directions.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Failure of antisymmetry]
Generated reachability on $S$ is not a partial order.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The distinct minor-third and major-third objects reach one another. Antisymmetry would identify them.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Mutual-reachability quotient]
The mutual-reachability classes are
\[
\{0\},\qquad\{3,4\},\qquad\{7,8,9\},\qquad\{12\}.
\]
Their quotient is a partial order, and these four classes are pairwise incomparable except for equality.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The complete table shows that $3,4$ form one strongly connected component, $7,8,9$ form another, and $0,12$ each have only a loop. No edge or path crosses between these components. Quotienting a preorder by mutual reachability is antisymmetric.
\end{proof}

Thus seven object names, fifteen local arrows, and four reachability classes are different invariants answering different questions.

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{algorithm}[Local motion enumeration]
For a finite list $V$ of dyads, examine every ordered pair $(x,y)$. Compute $\nu(x)$ and $\nu(y)$, test consonance, test both displacement bounds, and reject similar motion when both endpoints are perfect. Output exactly the accepted pairs.
\end{algorithm}
With constant-time arithmetic and finite-set tests, this takes $O(|V|^2)$ predicate evaluations and $O(|E|)$ output space.

Given the Boolean adjacency matrix $A$, initialize $R$ with $A$ and the identity, then for every intermediate vertex $k$ update
\[
R_{ij}\leftarrow R_{ij}\lor(R_{ik}\land R_{kj})
\]
for all $i,j$.

\begin{theorem}[Closure-algorithm correctness]
After all intermediate vertices are processed, $R_{ij}$ is true exactly when a finite permitted path connects $i$ to $j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the allowed intermediate vertices. Initially $R$ records paths of length zero or one. At stage $k$, a path either avoids $k$ or decomposes into a path from $i$ to $k$ and one from $k$ to $j$ using earlier intermediates. The update records exactly these alternatives.
\end{proof}

The matrix algorithm uses $O(|V|^3)$ time and $O(|V|^2)$ space. Repeated graph searches can instead use $O(|V|(|V|+|E|))$ time. Strongly connected components compute mutual-reachability classes in $O(|V|+|E|)$ time, and their condensation graph realizes the partial-order quotient.

\section{Interpretation and applications}
The analysis separates three levels. The local predicate is syntax: it decides whether one sonority may immediately follow another. The directed graph is dynamics: it displays immediate possibilities. Generated reachability is global structure: it records all destinations obtainable through finite paths. Composition naturally belongs to the third level.

If arrows mean single transitions, categorical closure fails. If arrows mean paths, the free path category preserves each itinerary. If arrows mean existence of paths, the thin category preserves accessibility while forgetting multiplicity. The thin model suits endpoint planning; the path model suits comparing realizations, counting alternatives, and assigning costs such as total melodic displacement.

The same distinction appears in robotics, rewriting systems, and reaction networks. A legal control, rewrite, or elementary reaction is nontransitive, while trajectory, derivability, or reaction-path reachability is transitive. Counterpoint makes the distinction audible.

The reversible thirds also explain a cost of quotienting. Minor and major thirds are musically distinct but merge in the reachability poset. A quotient may reveal coarse dynamics while erasing interval quality. The appropriate abstraction depends on the question.

\section{A complementary tree obstruction}
The same local-to-global principle yields a separate result for finite tree diagrams. Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph, with $\deg(v)$ the number of edges incident to $v$. A tree is connected and acyclic. Suppose that a singleton marking at $v$ is called $\rho$-dominant precisely when $\deg(v)\ge2$. In the motivating weight notation, the correction is $\lambda_{\{v\},I}=2\rho-\beta_I-\alpha_v$, and dominance means membership in the dominant cone $P^+$.

\begin{theorem}[Tree degree sum]
If $G$ is a tree on $n$ vertices, then
\[
\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)=2(n-1).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A finite tree on $n$ vertices has $n-1$ edges. The handshaking identity counts every edge once at each endpoint, so the degree sum is twice the edge count.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Leaf existence]
Every nonempty finite tree has a vertex $v$ with $\deg(v)\le1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If every vertex had degree at least two, the degree sum would be at least $2n$. The tree degree-sum theorem gives $2(n-1)<2n$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Leaf obstruction to dominant singletons]
Every nonempty tree diagram satisfying the singleton criterion above contains a vertex $v$ for which $\lambda_{\{v\},I}$ is not $\rho$-dominant.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose a leaf. Its degree is at most one, whereas singleton dominance is equivalent to degree at least two.
\end{proof}

Thus a forest condition forces forbidden local marking data. The result echoes the counterpoint analysis: global structure constrains local eligibility.

\section{Limitations}
The model uses semitone magnitude rather than diatonic spelling, permits voice crossing in principle, and lacks a fixed cantus, finite register, longer melodic constraints, and special opening and closing formulas. Similar motion excludes oblique motion and prohibits every similar transition between perfect consonances, without finer historical distinctions among parallel, direct, or repeated perfect intervals.

The canonical model additionally freezes the bass at zero, so it studies interval adjacency rather than a complete composition. Its edges never trigger the similar-perfect prohibition. The fifteen-edge theorem is exact for these representatives, not for every registered dyad.

A twelve-element conjecture remains underdetermined until twelve states are defined. Scale degree, interval quality, register, boundary role, and approach direction can enlarge the state space. Any equivalence claim must specify objects and arrows before cardinality can be tested.

\section{Future research}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Diatonic-state quotient.} Extend each canonical state by one of seven scale degrees, use the C-major step pattern, compute reachability, and quotient by mutual reachability. Test whether twelve classes result.
\item \textbf{Boundary-rule thinness.} Add perfect opening and closing consonances and contrary or oblique approach to the final. For fixed short cantus firmi in finite register, compare complete admissible counterpoints under pointwise reachability.
\item \textbf{Connectivity.} Test richer canonical rules for mutual reachability of all seven representatives. Under the current rule, $0$ and $12$ are isolated and the thirds and $7$--$9$ cluster are separate.
\item \textbf{Register sensitivity.} Restrict dyads to finite registers at least an octave wide, quotient by translation where possible, and identify boundary-sensitive differences.
\item \textbf{Parallel-perfect repair.} Refine similar motion to treat repeated perfect consonances under nonzero equal-direction motion explicitly, then recompute the canonical edge count.
\end{enumerate}
Weighted and non-thin extensions should also count paths, impose length bounds, and optimize musical costs.

\section{Conclusion}
The chain
\[
(0,3)\longrightarrow(2,5)\longrightarrow(4,7)
\]
contains two permitted motions, while its direct displacement is not permitted. Immediate motions therefore cannot be exactly the arrows of a thin category. Finite-path reachability supplies the canonical repair: it is reflexive and transitive and defines a thin category containing every direct move.

In the standard interval-only restriction there are exactly seven objects and fifteen canonical one-step motions. Reversible edges make reachability a preorder rather than a partial order until strongly connected states are quotiented. The methodological conclusion is that local musical rules generate categorical structure; they are not automatically categorical. Once a step is distinguished from a journey, counterpoint, graph reachability, order theory, and category theory align.

